<H2><FONT COLOR="#D60021">5.6.3.7.</FONT></H2>
</TD>
<TD VALIGN=top>
<H2>Dex Contour Plot</H2>
</TD>
</TR >
</TABLE>
</TD>
</TR>



<tr>
<td VALIGN=TOP WIDTH="15%">
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<i>
DEX Contour Plot
</i>
</td>

<td WIDTH="85%">
<!-- Add main text below -->
In order to determine the best settings for the factors, we can use
a dex contour plot. The dex contour plot shows the value of the
response variable at the vertices (i.e, the -1 and +1 settings for
the factor variables) and indicates the direction that maximizes or
minimizes the response variable. The dex contour plot is shown for
exactly 2 factor variables. If you have more than 2 significant
factors, you may need to generate a series of dex contour plots
(each using 2 of the important factors).
<p>
The following is the dex contour plot of the X4 (Arm Length)
and Number of Bands (X3).
<p>
<a href="catapult/gifs/dexcon_f.gif">
<img SRC="catapult/gifs/dexcon_r.gif"
     ALT="The dex contour plot identifies X3=1 and X4=1 as the optimal settings"
     BORDER=0 height=425 width=550>
</a>
<p>
The maximum value of the response variable (distance) corresponds
to X4 (Arm Length) equal to +1 and X3 (Number of Bands) equal to
+1. The thickened line in the contour plot corresponds to the direction
that maximizes the response variable.
</td>
